var BANK_PARTS = BANK_PARTS || [];
BANK_PARTS.push([
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":2,"s":"Analyst Reza argues, with delivery figures attached, that the co-op should drop its overnight contract. Reza's spouse owns a warehouse that would gain clients if the contract ended. Citing only that fact, the board dismisses the figures and concludes the contract should be kept.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats Reza's spouse as an authority on whether overnight contracts should be kept","infers that the contract is sound merely because the contract and the co-op's profits once rose together","rejects the figures because of who would gain if the contract ended, rather than because of a defect in the figures","concludes that every co-op should keep overnight delivery from the case of this one co-op","assumes that a feature of each delivery is a feature of the contract as a whole"],"a":2,"e":"The only ground offered for setting the figures aside is the spouse's stake. The stake is asked to do the work of a rebuttal. The attractive choice — treats Reza's spouse as an authority on whether overnight contracts should be kept — loses because the board does not rely on the spouse's expertise. It treats the spouse's possible gain as a reason to ignore Reza's figures."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each page of the 1840 census volume is intact enough to be photographed flat. Therefore, the bound volume is intact enough to be photographed as one object on the archive's book cradle.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the bound volume has a feature merely because each page has that feature","takes a condition required for photographing a page to be enough to guarantee photographing the volume","rejects the archivist because the archivist sells souvenir prints","treats the lack of a damage report as proof that no page is torn","infers that photography caused the census to survive merely because both are mentioned"],"a":0,"e":"Flat pages can still make a book that will not open on a cradle. The feature of the parts is handed to the bound whole. The attractive choice — takes a condition required for photographing a page to be enough to guarantee photographing the volume — loses because no photographing requirement is stated, and none is treated as a guarantee. The leap is from pages that can be shot flat to a bound book on a cradle."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Complaints were 30 percent of help-desk tickets last quarter and 12 percent this quarter. The manager concludes that the desk received fewer complaints this quarter.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["assumes that the share of complaints will keep falling next quarter","rejects the tally because of the manager's bonus","treats a feature of each complaint as a feature of the whole desk","overlooks the possibility that total tickets grew enough to offset complaints' smaller share","slides between two meanings of the word \"complaint\""],"a":3,"e":"A smaller share of a larger pile can be more complaints. The argument moves from share to number without a total. The attractive choice — assumes that the share of complaints will keep falling next quarter — loses because the conclusion is a count for this quarter, not a forecast that the share will continue to fall. The two-quarter comparison is being read as a change in number."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"Anyone who files a complete homestead claim receives a brass marker. Ellis has a brass marker. Therefore, Ellis filed a complete homestead claim.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the marker caused Ellis to file, because the marker and a filing are both in the argument","treats a condition sufficient for receiving a marker as though it were the only way to receive one","generalizes from Ellis to every homesteader in the territory","rejects the marker system because the clerk might profit from brass sales","treats two kinds of land claims as the only kinds that exist"],"a":1,"e":"Filing is enough for a marker. Having a marker is then treated as enough to show Ellis filed. That reverses a one-way rule. The attractive choice — infers that the marker caused Ellis to file, because the marker and a filing are both in the argument — loses because the argument runs from the marker back to the filing. It does not claim the marker caused the filing."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Shoppers who redeemed a premium coupon were asked about the store's advice, and most rated it highly. The retailer concludes that most of its shoppers rate that advice highly.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["overlooks the possibility that the coupon caused the high ratings","treats a condition needed to redeem a coupon as though it guaranteed a high rating","lets the survey and the store's advertisements certify each other","infers that the whole chain has a feature shown only for one aisle","takes coupon redeemers to be representative of the store's shoppers on the question of advice"],"a":4,"e":"The people asked had already redeemed a premium coupon. Their praise is then assigned to shoppers as a group. The attractive choice — overlooks the possibility that the coupon caused the high ratings — loses because the conclusion is not that the coupon produced the ratings. It is that redeemers speak for shoppers generally. A causal aside is not the sampling step."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"The curator argues: either the sketches are locked in the vault overnight, or they will be stolen. They will not be locked in the vault. Therefore, they will be stolen.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the whole museum is unsafe because the sketch gallery is unsafe","dismisses a guard's report because the guard wants overtime","treats a night in the vault and theft as the only possibilities","treats last year's theft-free nights as proof about every future night","uses \"stolen\" to mean borrowed in the premise and taken permanently in the conclusion"],"a":2,"e":"Refusing the vault is treated as theft. That holds only if nothing else could keep the sketches safe. The attractive choice — infers that the whole museum is unsafe because the sketch gallery is unsafe — loses because the argument is about these sketches, not about the museum as a whole. The flaw is the missing third option, not a parts-to-whole leap."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A nationally known cellist says the town's drinking wells should be dug twenty feet deeper. Therefore, the wells should be dug twenty feet deeper.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats a cellist's remark as sufficient to settle a question of well depth","rejects the water board's measurements because the board might gain from deeper wells","infers the depth from a correlation between concerts and water use","generalizes from one well to a conclusion that was already about the town's wells, which matches the cellist's own scope","slides between \"deeper\" as farther down and \"deeper\" as more expensive"],"a":0,"e":"Well depth is inferred from musical fame. Nothing shows the cellist applied a relevant standard to these wells. The attractive choice — rejects the water board's measurements because the board might gain from deeper wells — loses because no board is attacked, and no measurement is dismissed. The only premise is the cellist's remark."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No patrol note mentions a crack in the east seawall. The harbor master concludes that the east seawall has no crack.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["takes for granted that \"crack\" in a patrol note means a legal violation rather than a split in the wall","infers that every wall in the harbor is sound because the east wall is said to be sound","dismisses the patrol because the patrol captain rents boats","treats the absence of patrol notes as proof that no crack exists","projects last year's notes onto next year as the flaw, though the conclusion is about the wall now"],"a":3,"e":"Notes that never mention a crack are being asked to prove the wall is intact. That asks silence to be a search. The attractive choice — takes for granted that \"crack\" in a patrol note means a legal violation rather than a split in the wall — loses because the argument does not trade on two meanings. It treats missing notes as a structural finding."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Post hoc","d":3,"s":"The chapel bell was oiled on Monday. On Tuesday the bell rang late. The sexton concludes that oiling the bell caused the late ringing.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["fails to consider that a late bell is required before the chapel allows oiling","infers that oiling caused the late ringing merely because the oiling came first","dismisses the sexton because the sexton sells oil","treats one late ring as proof that every bell in the diocese rings late","slides between \"late\" as behind schedule and \"late\" as recently deceased"],"a":1,"e":"Monday's oiling and Tuesday's late ring are in sequence, and sequence is asked to prove cause. Nothing else connects them. The attractive choice — fails to consider that a late bell is required before the chapel allows oiling — loses because no such chapel rule is used. The argument's only link is that oiling preceded the late ring, and that order is treated as a cause."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Hasty generalization","d":3,"s":"The first three fossils catalogued from the north quarry were ammonites. The curator concludes that the quarry's fossils are ammonites.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the cataloguing caused the fossils to be ammonites","rejects a rival curator because of a museum salary","slides between \"ammonite\" as a fossil and \"ammonite\" as a living squid","treats two geological eras as the only eras that could be in the quarry","draws a conclusion about the quarry's fossils from the first three that were catalogued"],"a":4,"e":"Three early catalogue entries are a small, possibly ordered slice. They are asked to represent the quarry's fossils. The attractive choice — infers that the cataloguing caused the fossils to be ammonites — loses because the curator is not offering a cause of the fossils' type. The curator is treating three items as the quarry."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"The studio spotlight went dark during rehearsal. The stage manager concludes that the bulb has burned out.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["A spotlight with a new bulb cannot go dark.","Burned-out bulbs are more common on stages than in lobbies.","No cause other than a burned-out bulb accounts for the spotlight going dark.","The rehearsal continued after the spotlight went dark.","The stage manager has replaced bulbs in this theater before."],"a":2,"e":"Darkness does not pick out a burned-out bulb if a breaker, a switch, or a loose cable remains available. The conclusion depends on those rivals being out. The attractive choice — A spotlight with a new bulb cannot go dark. — loses because the argument does not need the bulb to be new, and it does not need new bulbs to be incapable of darkness. It needs other causes of this darkness ruled out. A new bulb that can still go dark is compatible with the conclusion if this bulb did burn out and nothing else did the work."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The cedar footbridge has carried every harvest float for a decade. The marshal concludes that it will carry this year's float.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["This year's float is not relevantly harder on the bridge than the floats already carried.","The bridge will carry every float that is heavier than the floats already carried.","The parade has always used this bridge rather than the main road.","The float uses the same colors every year.","Crowd estimates for the parade have been reliable."],"a":0,"e":"If this float is relevantly harsher than the earlier ones, the decade does not reach it. The projection depends on that harshness being absent. The attractive choice — The bridge will carry every float that is heavier than the floats already carried. — loses because that claim is stronger than the conclusion and is not needed for it. The marshal needs this float not to be a harder case. A promise about heavier floats can be false while this comparable float is still supported."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each pump in the plaza fountain is under the town's quiet limit. The engineer concludes that the fountain as a whole is under that limit.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The fountain is under the quiet limit only if each pump is under the limit.","The plaza also contains a noisy market.","One pump was replaced earlier this year.","The fountain is no louder than its loudest pump, and no other part of it adds sound.","The quiet limit was adopted by the town council."],"a":3,"e":"If the basin or the combination of pumps adds sound, quiet pumps do not yield a quiet fountain. The conclusion depends on no such addition. The attractive choice — The fountain is under the quiet limit only if each pump is under the limit. — loses because that \"only if\" runs from the whole to the parts. The argument runs from the parts to the whole. The parts being quiet is already the premise. What is required is that quiet parts add up to a quiet fountain, not that a quiet fountain requires quiet parts."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":3,"s":"Engineer Okada argues that the elm footbridge should close and cites a crack measurement. Because Okada's cousin gains boat riders when the bridge closes, the council treats the measurement as void and concludes the bridge need not close.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Okada's cousin gains more riders whenever the bridge is closed.","A family stake in boat ridership is, by itself, a reason to treat Okada's measurement as void.","Boat taxis in the area have lost riders this year.","Some residents prefer the view from the bridge.","Okada lives several miles from the footbridge."],"a":1,"e":"The council's only step from the measurement to \"need not close\" is the cousin's gain. That step depends on the gain being enough to set the measurement aside. The attractive choice — Okada's cousin gains more riders whenever the bridge is closed. — loses because that gain is already the council's premise. Restating it is not a missing assumption. The missing piece is that the gain is enough to void the measurement. If a relative's gain does not void a measurement, the council's dismissal fails."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":3,"s":"Offices that started a lunchtime walk reported fewer afternoon fatigue complaints than offices that did not. The director concludes that the walks reduced the complaints.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Offices whose staff were already less tired were the ones that started the walks.","The walks take twenty minutes.","The director enjoys walking.","Some offices without walks also improved slightly.","The walk offices and the other offices did not differ, aside from the walks, in a way that accounts for the fatigue gap."],"a":4,"e":"A prior difference that tracks the fatigue gap would leave the walks unproven. The causal conclusion depends on no such difference doing that work. The attractive choice — Offices whose staff were already less tired were the ones that started the walks. — loses because that is a selection story. If it is true, the walks are a poor candidate for the cause. The argument does not depend on assuming the story that undermines it. It depends on no such story being the real account."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"This bus route has arrived on time every weekday this season. The dispatcher concludes that it will arrive on time next weekday.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The route will be on time on every weekday next season as well.","The route uses the same bus number it used last year.","Nothing about next weekday makes a delay relevantly more likely than on the weekdays already observed.","Riders prefer buses that arrive on time.","Fares on the route rose last month."],"a":2,"e":"If next weekday adds a new source of delay, the season's record does not reach it. The forecast depends on no such source. The attractive choice — The route will be on time on every weekday next season as well. — loses because next season is beyond the conclusion. The dispatcher needs one coming weekday not to be a harder case. A promise about a later season can fail while that one weekday is still like the ones already run."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A noted violinist, citing no well-depth rule, says the town well should be dug ten feet deeper. The council concludes from that remark alone that the well should be dug ten feet deeper.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The violinist's judgment is a reliable guide to how deep this well should be dug.","The violinist has performed at the town festival.","Wells in the next county are deeper than this well.","The council meets on Tuesdays.","Some residents own the violinist's recordings."],"a":0,"e":"If a violinist's judgment is not a reliable guide to this well, the council's only premise fails to support the depth. The argument depends on that reliability. The attractive choice — The violinist has performed at the town festival. — loses because a local performance is extra fame. The argument already has the violinist's name. It does not depend on a festival credit. It depends on that person's judgment actually being a guide to this well, which a concert does not supply and which the conclusion cannot survive without."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Blocks that added evening streetlights had fewer collisions that season than blocks that did not. Therefore, the streetlights caused the drop in collisions.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Drivers prefer well-lit blocks to dark ones.","Streetlights use electricity.","Some unlit blocks also had fewer collisions than they had the year before.","If one set of blocks had extra streetlights and fewer collisions than another set, and no other relevant difference is given, then the streetlights caused the drop.","The lights were installed in October."],"a":3,"e":"The premises give only the gap. The conditional says that this kind of gap is causal. Together they entail that the lights caused the drop. The attractive choice — Drivers prefer well-lit blocks to dark ones. — loses because a preference does not, with the collision gap alone, force the lights to be the cause. Drivers can prefer lights while the gap comes from speed limits or traffic volume. The bridging conditional is what makes the conclusion follow."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"No log reports a leak in the blue canoe. Therefore, the blue canoe has no leak.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Leaks are expensive to repair.","Every leak in the blue canoe would have been reported in the log, and the log is complete.","The blue canoe is rented more often than the red one.","The outfitter has repaired leaks in other canoes this season.","The log is written in ink."],"a":1,"e":"If any leak would be in a complete log and the log is empty of leaks, there was no leak. That pair is enough for the conclusion. The attractive choice — Leaks are expensive to repair. — loses because cost does not turn silence into a clean hull. A complete log that would have caught any leak, together with the silence, does."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":3,"s":"The trees will not be netted. Therefore, the crop will be ruined.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["More birds are nesting in the orchard this season than last season.","Netting is expensive.","The orchard is old.","Some crops in the valley are netted.","If the trees are not netted, the crop will be ruined."],"a":4,"e":"The trees will not be netted. The conditional says that is enough for ruin. The conclusion follows. The attractive choice — More birds are nesting in the orchard this season than last season. — loses because that birds eat fruit does not, with the refusal to net, force ruin. A conditional from skipping the nets to ruin does force it."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Equivocation","d":2,"s":"The gardener called the soil rich. The nursery's \"rich\" means that no fertilizer is needed. Therefore, this soil needs no fertilizer.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["The gardener spoke in the morning.","The nursery sells fertilizer.","The gardener uses \"rich\" in the nursery's sense.","Soil can look dark and still need fertilizer.","Some soils called rich are sandy."],"a":2,"e":"The remark says the soil is rich. Matching that word to the nursery's definition yields \"no fertilizer.\" The attractive choice — The gardener spoke in the morning. — loses because a morning remark does not align the two senses. If the gardener's \"rich\" is the nursery's \"rich,\" the conclusion follows from the remark."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Members who switched to slow pottery wheels reported less wrist pain than members who kept the old wheels. The head concludes that the slow wheels reduced wrist pain.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Before the switch, the two groups had similar pain and similar hours, and neither group changed its clay or its schedule.","Potters who already had comfortable wrists were more likely to switch to the slow wheels.","The slow wheels cost more to repair than the old wheels.","The studio sells finished pots to a shop down the street.","A few potters on the old wheels also improved a little, though less than the switchers."],"a":0,"e":"Matching pain, hours, clay, and schedule beforehand removes the obvious rivals, so the wheel change is better support for the causal claim. The attractive choice — Potters who already had comfortable wrists were more likely to switch to the slow wheels. — loses because it uses the right groups and the wrong direction. If the switchers were already more comfortable, the later gap has another explanation and the causal claim is weaker, not stronger."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Postcards fell from 20 percent of gift-shop items sold to 10 percent. The buyer concludes that fewer postcards were sold.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The later period included a holiday weekend and the earlier period did not.","Postcards take up more shelf space than enamel pins.","Some visitors bought two postcards.","The shop sold the same number of items in both periods.","The shop added a line of stuffed fish during the later period."],"a":3,"e":"Same total, half the share, fewer postcards. The holiday choice never locks the total. The attractive choice — The later period included a holiday weekend and the earlier period did not. — loses because a holiday mentions both periods and feels like a control. It leaves the total free to rise, so the number of postcards need not have fallen. A fixed total is what turns the share drop into a count drop."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"The gardener called the soil rich. Using the nursery's sense of \"rich,\" a clerk concludes that the soil needs no fertilizer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["By \"rich,\" the gardener meant only that the soil looks dark.","By \"rich,\" the gardener meant soil that needs no added fertilizer.","In ordinary gardening talk, soil called rich can still need fertilizer.","The nursery sells fertilizer beside the soil bins.","The gardener made the remark last month."],"a":1,"e":"If the gardener's sense is the nursery's sense, the conclusion follows from the remark. The dark-soil gloss does the opposite. The attractive choice — By \"rich,\" the gardener meant only that the soil looks dark. — loses because the dark-soil reading uses the same word and pulls the senses apart. That widens the gap the clerk jumped. The matching definition closes it."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":3,"s":"Either the trees are netted or the crop will be ruined. They will not be netted. Therefore, the crop will be ruined.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Birds are more numerous in the orchard this season than last season.","Netting is the most expensive option the orchard has priced.","The manager dislikes the look of netting.","Some buyers prefer fruit from netted trees.","This orchard will use no bird protection other than netting, and orchards that skip netting without another protection lose the crop."],"a":4,"e":"If there is no other protection and unprotected orchards lose the crop, refusing the nets yields ruin. Extra birds never close that gap. The attractive choice — Birds are more numerous in the orchard this season than last season. — loses because more birds feel like trouble for the fruit, so they seem to help \"ruin.\" They do not show that skipping netting leaves no other protection. The no-other-protection fact is what makes the two options exhaustive."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Anyone who finishes the dock course receives a green badge. Riley has a green badge. A clerk concludes that Riley finished the course.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Riley was seen carrying dock-course materials the week the badge was issued.","The course is offered only on weekdays.","Green badges are issued only to people who finish the dock course, and Riley's badge is one of those badges.","Most people who finish the course wear the badge on a lanyard.","The course was shortened last year."],"a":2,"e":"If this kind of badge goes only to finishers, Riley's badge means Riley finished. Being seen with a handbook does not force that. The attractive choice — Riley was seen carrying dock-course materials the week the badge was issued. — loses because course materials make it look as if Riley attended, so they seem to confirm the clerk. They do not close the logical gap: a badge can still come from another route. \"Issued only to finishers\" closes it. Materials are compatible with Riley never finishing."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Offices that added lunchtime walks later had fewer fatigue complaints than offices that did not. The director concludes that the walks reduced fatigue.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Before the walks, both groups had the same fatigue rates and the same workloads, and nothing else about the afternoons changed.","The offices that added walks had already cut their workloads in half.","The walks take about twenty minutes.","The director personally enjoys walking.","Some offices without walks improved slightly."],"a":0,"e":"Equal fatigue and equal workloads beforehand, with no other afternoon change, leave the walks as the remaining difference that tracks the gap. The attractive choice — The offices that added walks had already cut their workloads in half. — loses because a workload cut only in the walk offices is an alternative cause sitting in the same comparison. It looks like a careful control and is in fact a rival explanation, so it weakens the walks."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No tester found a crack in the blue canoe's hull. The outfitter concludes that the hull had no crack.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The outfitter has a reputation for careful work.","The blue canoe is rented more often than the red canoe.","Cracks are costly to repair.","The test used would have revealed any crack this hull could have, and the whole hull was tested.","Testers say they prefer blue boats."],"a":3,"e":"A test that would have shown any crack, run over the whole hull, turns a negative result into evidence of no crack. The attractive choice — The outfitter has a reputation for careful work. — loses because a reputation feels like support for a clean hull and never shows that this test would have caught a crack. Sensitivity plus full coverage does."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":3,"s":"The regional water engineer who wrote the town's well manual says this well should be dug ten feet deeper, applying the manual to the latest measurements. The council concludes that it should be dug ten feet deeper.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The engineer plays the violin at the town festival.","The manual's depth rules are the standard the council uses, and the measurements were taken correctly.","Wells in the next county are deeper.","The council meets on Tuesdays.","Residents say they enjoy the engineer's public lectures."],"a":1,"e":"A correct measurement fed into the council's own depth rule is support for digging. A festival violin is not that rule. The attractive choice — The engineer plays the violin at the town festival. — loses because a musical hobby adds a second kind of fame and does not confirm that the manual is the council's rule or that the measurements were right. Those two facts are what make the application a reason for this council."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":3,"s":"Either the trees are netted or the birds will ruin the crop. They will not be netted. Therefore, the birds will ruin the crop.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Birds are more numerous this season than last season.","Netting is the most expensive protection on the price list.","The manager has long disliked the look of netting.","Some buyers prefer fruit grown under netting.","Orchards that skipped netting and used reflective tape kept their crops, and this orchard will use that tape."],"a":4,"e":"The inference needs netting and ruin to be the only options. Tape that saves crops, and that this orchard will use, is a third option. The attractive choice — Birds are more numerous this season than last season. — loses because more birds sound like a threat to the fruit and seem to help the manager's grim conclusion. They do not open a third option. Tape that saves an unnetted crop does."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Bikes fell from 40 percent of garage vehicles to 25 percent. Therefore, the garage holds fewer bikes.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Twenty-five percent is less than 40 percent.","Bikes take less space than cars.","The garage holds twice as many vehicles this year as last year.","Some bikes are stored on racks.","The garage was repainted between the two counts."],"a":2,"e":"Double the vehicles and a share of 25 percent can be more bikes than 40 percent of the old total. The count conclusion does not survive that total. The attractive choice — Twenty-five percent is less than 40 percent. — loses because the smaller share is the premise, restated as if it finished the job. Restating it does not show the number fell. Twice the vehicles can raise the number of bikes while the share drops."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A tablet ban reduced noise in the archive reading room. Therefore, a tablet ban will reduce noise in the clinic waiting room.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Archive noise was mostly tablet audio, while waiting-room noise is mostly door chimes and carts, and tablets there are already silent.","The archive bought new chairs in the same month the ban began.","The archive has carpets and the waiting room has tile.","The clinic building is larger than the archive.","Some visitors dislike tablet bans."],"a":0,"e":"If the waiting room's noise is not tablet noise, a tablet ban that worked on tablet noise is a weak reason to expect a quieter waiting room. The attractive choice — The archive bought new chairs in the same month the ban began. — loses because new chairs muddy the archive result and feel like a reason to doubt the premise. They do not show that waiting-room noise has a source the ban cannot touch. Chimes, carts, and silent tablets do."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each of the four instrument cases is light enough for one person to carry on. Therefore, the full set is light enough for one person to carry on.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Each case has its own handle.","The quartet travels by streetcar rather than by road.","The four cases are the same color.","Together the four cases weigh more than the streetcar allows one person to carry.","One of the cases holds a violin."],"a":3,"e":"Four liftable cases can still make an illegal armful. A total over the streetcar's one-person limit is that armful. The attractive choice — Each case has its own handle. — loses because a handle on each case echoes the premise that each case can be carried. It feels like support for carrying the set and says nothing about the combined weight. The combined weight over the limit blocks the conclusion."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The bus has been on time every weekday this season. Therefore, it will be on time next weekday.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Fares on the route rose last month.","Next weekday the route adds a drawbridge that has been stopping traffic for twenty minutes at a time.","The bus keeps the same route number.","Riders prefer buses that arrive on time.","The dispatcher has held the job for years."],"a":1,"e":"A twenty-minute bridge delay is a new obstacle the on-time record did not include. The projection to next weekday loses its likeness. The attractive choice — Fares on the route rose last month. — loses because a fare change feels like a break in the season's conditions. It is not a reason the bus would miss its time. A new drawbridge delay is."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"Grit stopped puddles on the studio path. Therefore, grit will stop puddles on the rooftop track.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The track is on a roof and the path is on the ground.","Grit is sold in bags.","Runners dislike puddles.","The path is outdoors in summer and winter.","Path puddles come from foot traffic packing the soil, while track puddles come from a roof deck with no drain, and grit does not add a drain."],"a":4,"e":"The path problem is packed soil and the track problem is drainage. A remedy for the first need not touch the second. The attractive choice — The track is on a roof and the path is on the ground. — loses because naming the roof only restates that the settings differ, which a quick reader hears as \"analogy fails.\" A bare location is not yet a difference that matters. A missing drain that grit does not fix is that difference."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Complaints fell from 30 percent of help-desk tickets to 10 percent. The manager concludes that the desk received fewer complaints.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Ten percent is less than 30 percent.","The desk hired one new agent.","The desk received five times as many tickets in the later period.","Some tickets are compliments rather than complaints.","Complaints are filed on the same form as other tickets."],"a":2,"e":"Ten percent of a pile five times larger is more complaints than 30 percent of the old pile. The count conclusion fails. The attractive choice — Ten percent is less than 30 percent. — loses because restating the smaller share feels like the arithmetic is finished. It is the premise. Five times as many tickets can raise the number of complaints while the share falls."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"A tent on the green can host the festival without canceling it and without moving it into the civic hall. The director says the only ways to hold the festival are to cancel it or to move it into the civic hall.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The director's claim that those are the only two ways is false.","The festival will be held in the tent.","The civic hall cannot hold the festival.","The festival will be canceled.","The director made no claim about the civic hall."],"a":0,"e":"A live third option falsifies the claim that cancellation and the hall are the only ways. It does not select the tent, close the hall, or predict cancellation. The attractive choice — The festival will be held in the tent. — loses because the tent shows a third way, so \"only two ways\" is false. A quick reading then books the tent. The statements do not say the festival will use it."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A recommendation about bridge cables is not established merely because a celebrated pastry chef makes it. The only support offered for replacing the cables is a celebrated pastry chef's recommendation.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The cables should not be replaced.","Pastry chefs know nothing about bridges.","An engineer has inspected the cables.","The support offered does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","A recommendation by an engineer would also fail to establish that the cables should be replaced."],"a":3,"e":"The chef's recommendation is the only support, and that kind of citation does not establish a cable recommendation. The cables themselves are not judged. The attractive choice — The cables should not be replaced. — loses because the principle knocks out this citation. It does not say the cables are fine. \"Should not be replaced\" is the practical conclusion a reader may want, and it is stronger than the failure of this one citation."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"A smaller share of a total establishes a smaller number only if the total did not grow enough to offset the smaller share. Bikes' share of the garage fell, and the garage now holds twice as many vehicles. Nothing else is said about the number of bikes.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The garage holds more bikes than it held before.","The drop in bikes' share does not, by itself, establish that the garage holds fewer bikes.","The garage holds fewer bikes than it held before.","Bikes are now 25 percent of the vehicles.","No cars were added to the garage."],"a":1,"e":"The offset condition fails because the total doubled, so the principle says the share drop does not establish fewer bikes. It does not establish more bikes either. The attractive choice — The garage holds more bikes than it held before. — loses because twice the vehicles means the share drop does not settle the count. It does not compute a larger count either, because the old and new shares are not both given as numbers that force the direction. \"More bikes\" is the flashy arithmetic. \"Not established\" is what follows."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":3,"s":"Joint occurrence, by itself, does not establish causation. Honey sales and a new weekend market rose in the same weeks. No other fact about a cause is given.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The market did not cause the honey-sales rise.","The market caused the honey-sales rise.","Honey sales rose for some reason other than the market.","The market sold no honey.","Those two rises do not, by themselves, establish that the market caused the honey-sales rise."],"a":4,"e":"Only joint occurrence is given, and joint occurrence does not establish causation. So the market is not shown to be the cause. It is also not shown not to be. The attractive choice — The market did not cause the honey-sales rise. — loses because the principle says the pair of rises is not enough to prove causation. It does not prove the market innocent. \"Did not cause\" is the strong denial. \"Do not establish\" is the result on the page."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"In three separate years, the harbor festival that hired extra streetlights had fewer evening collisions on the festival blocks than in the years it did not. No other difference among those years is described.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The streetlights were the only possible cause of the fewer collisions.","The festival should be canceled.","The extra streetlights are a serious candidate for explaining the fewer collisions in the years they were used.","Collisions rose in every year the festival was unlit.","Streetlights have no bearing on collisions."],"a":2,"e":"The lights are present in the lower-collision years and absent in the comparison years, with no described rival. That supports treating them as a candidate. It does not crown them the only possible cause. The attractive choice — The streetlights were the only possible cause of the fewer collisions. — loses because three matching years make the lights a live candidate. They do not close every other cause. \"Only possible cause\" is the causal conclusion in stronger clothes than the pattern will bear."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Complaints fell from 30 percent of help-desk tickets to 10 percent, and the desk received the same number of tickets in both periods.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The desk received fewer complaints in the later period.","Every ticket that was not a complaint was a compliment.","The desk received fewer tickets in the later period.","The share of complaints rose.","The desk hired no new agents."],"a":0,"e":"Same total, smaller share, fewer complaints. That count follows. The character of the other tickets does not. The attractive choice — Every ticket that was not a complaint was a compliment. — loses because the arithmetic supports fewer complaints. It does not classify the other tickets. \"Compliment\" is a vivid label for the remainder, and nothing says the remainder was praise."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"The engineer who wrote the town's well manual applied its depth rule to this well's latest measurements, which were taken correctly, and recommended digging ten feet deeper. The council treats that manual as its standard.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Every recommendation by any engineer is correct.","The well should be closed instead of deepened.","Measurements never matter to well depth.","The recommendation is on point for the depth decision the council is making.","The manual is about concert halls."],"a":3,"e":"Manual, correct measurement, and the council's own standard line up with the advice. That supports \"on point.\" It does not support a universal trust in engineers. The attractive choice — Every recommendation by any engineer is correct. — loses because a correct measurement fed into the council's own rule makes this recommendation on point. It does not make every engineer infallible. The attractive choice keeps the expert and inflates the claim from this decision to all engineers."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":3,"s":"It did not rain, the path drains within an hour, the path was underwater this morning, and an open irrigation line is the only other way that path floods. The valve's position was not written down.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The groundskeeper forgot to close the valve.","The facts point more strongly to an open irrigation line than to rain.","Rain flooded the path.","The path cannot flood.","Every garden path in the county was underwater."],"a":1,"e":"Rain is out, and the only other listed cause is an open line. That ranks the open line above rain. It does not identify who left it open. The attractive choice — The groundskeeper forgot to close the valve. — loses because rain is ruled out and the remaining listed cause is an open line, so the open line is the stronger reading. \"The groundskeeper forgot\" adds a person and a mental state the facts do not name. The valve could have been left open by someone else."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"The gardener and the nursery both use \"rich\" to mean soil that needs no added fertilizer. The gardener called this soil rich.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The gardener meant only that the soil looks dark.","The soil needs twice the usual fertilizer.","The nursery never uses the word \"rich.\"","Dark soil always needs fertilizer.","On that shared meaning, the gardener's remark supports the claim that this soil needs no added fertilizer."],"a":4,"e":"Same definition, plus the gardener's remark, supports \"no added fertilizer.\" A dark-soil gloss is a different meaning from the one stated. The attractive choice — The gardener meant only that the soil looks dark. — loses because the statements fix the shared meaning as \"no added fertilizer.\" The dark-soil reading is the ordinary trap for this word, and it contradicts the meaning just given."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"The elm pilings fail the manual's thickness rule. Feldman applies that rule and concludes that the elm pilings should be replaced this year. The manual is the board's standard.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The elm pilings fail the manual's thickness rule.","The manual is the board's standard.","The elm pilings should be replaced this year.","Every piling in the harbor should be replaced.","Feldman writes harbor manuals."],"a":2,"e":"The argument's point is the replacement conclusion. The thickness failure and the manual's status are why that conclusion is drawn, and \"every piling\" is wider than the elm pilings named. The attractive choice — The elm pilings fail the manual's thickness rule. — loses because failing the thickness rule is the evidence Feldman uses. The claim that evidence is offered to support is replacement this year. A premise in the conclusion's clothing is the usual miss on this stem."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Each case is light enough to carry, but a feature of each case need not belong to the set of cases. So the quartet has not shown that one person can carry the full set onto the streetcar.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The quartet has not shown that one person can carry the full set onto the streetcar.","Each case is light enough for one person to carry.","The full set is too heavy to carry onto the streetcar.","Streetcars ban instrument cases.","The quartet owns four cases."],"a":0,"e":"The argument stops at \"has not shown.\" It does not establish that the set is too heavy or that the streetcar has a ban. The attractive choice — Each case is light enough for one person to carry. — loses because the light cases are the premise. The \"so\" sentence is the conclusion, and it is about a missing proof, not about a proof that the set is too heavy and not about the cases one by one."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"The path was underwater, it did not rain, the path drains within an hour, and an open irrigation line is the only other way that path floods. So the irrigation line was left open.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The path was underwater this morning.","It did not rain overnight.","The path drains within an hour.","The irrigation line was left open.","Every path in town flooded."],"a":3,"e":"The other facts are arranged to support the open line, and the argument says so. It does not conclude that every path flooded. The attractive choice — The path was underwater this morning. — loses because the wet path is a premise. The conclusion is the open line. A striking fact at the start of the argument is often mistaken for the point."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":3,"s":"The chef's remark is the only support offered for replacing the cables, and a chef's say-so does not establish a cable decision. So the remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["A pastry chef commented on the cables.","The chef's remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","The cables should stay in place.","Cable decisions require engineering evidence, and the chef supplied that evidence.","The chef opposes replacement."],"a":1,"e":"The conclusion is the failure of this remark to establish replacement. It is not a finding that the cables are fine, and it does not say the chef supplied engineering evidence. The attractive choice — A pastry chef commented on the cables. — loses because that a chef spoke is setup. The point is that the remark fails as proof. The argument does not conclude that the cables should stay, which would be a stronger practical verdict."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":3,"s":"Honey sales and the new market rose in the same weeks. The clerk concludes that the market caused the sales rise.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that a word is used in one sense by the clerk and in another sense by the market vendors","attacking the clerk's motives for liking markets","citing a novelist as an authority on honey","breaking a percentage of sales into a count by fixing the total","inferring a cause from the fact that two things happened at the same time"],"a":4,"e":"The only link is that the rises coincided, and the conclusion is causal. That is the method. The attractive choice — showing that a word is used in one sense by the clerk and in another sense by the market vendors — loses because no word changes sense. The attractive description sounds technical and finds no second meaning on the page. The move is from joint timing to a cause."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"The critic's \"original\" means a new design. The rule's \"original\" means the first physical panel. The councillor treats the critic's word as if it satisfied the rule.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["appealing to the critic as a legal authority on which murals the rule protects","inferring a cause from a correlation between reviews and demolitions","using one sense of \"original\" as though it were the other sense","generalizing from a matinee audience","concluding that a past record of protection will repeat"],"a":2,"e":"Two meanings of \"original\" are treated as one. That equivocation is the method. The attractive choice — appealing to the critic as a legal authority on which murals the rule protects — loses because the critic is not cited as a judge of the rule. The attractive choice sees a critic and invents an appeal to authority. The slip is between two meanings of one word."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"The mileage log is correct because it matches the driver's memory, and the memory is correct because it matches the log.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["supporting each of two claims only by the other","citing a champion driver as an authority on odometers","polling one group of drivers and concluding about all drivers","banning phones in two different vehicles","noting that this year's load is heavier than last year's"],"a":0,"e":"Each of the log and the memory is the only support offered for the other. That is circular support. The attractive choice — citing a champion driver as an authority on odometers — loses because no champion is cited. The attractive choice upgrades the driver into an authority. The two claims are holding each other up, which is the method."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"The bridge carried ten years of floats, so it will carry this year's float. This year's weight is not compared with the earlier weights.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["rejecting a sample of floats as too narrow to represent all parades","showing that two options for the parade are not exhaustive","defining a badge rule and then running it backward","projecting a past record forward without comparing the relevant conditions","lifting each pane and then concluding about a stack because the stack has one pane"],"a":3,"e":"A decade of success is the only ground for this year, and the loads are not compared. That is a past-to-future projection. The attractive choice — rejecting a sample of floats as too narrow to represent all parades — loses because there is no sample of spectators or parades being generalized. The attractive choice sees \"ten years\" and calls it a sample-size flaw. The method is a streak pushed one year ahead."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":3,"s":"**Honey sales rose in the same weeks as the new market.** The clerk concludes from that overlap that the market caused the rise.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the conclusion of the argument.","It is the only premise offered for the causal conclusion.","It is an objection the clerk rejects.","It is a principle that would license any causal claim.","It is evidence that the market did not cause the rise."],"a":1,"e":"The overlap is the sole support. The conclusion is the causal claim that follows it. The attractive choice — It is the conclusion of the argument. — loses because the overlap is not what the clerk is trying to establish. The causal claim comes after it. Calling the premise the conclusion is the standard role reversal."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":3,"s":"The rule protects the first physical panel. **The critic called the design new.** The councillor treats that remark as if it satisfied the rule.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the town rule itself.","It is the conclusion that the mural is protected.","It is an objection the critic raises against the rule.","It is evidence that no design can be new.","It is a premise that uses a different sense from the rule the conclusion relies on."],"a":4,"e":"The bold sentence is the critic's description, used as a premise. The rule is the other sentence. The protection claim is the conclusion, and it is not bolded. The attractive choice — It is the town rule itself. — loses because the critic's remark is easy to merge with the rule because both are about \"original\" work. The remark is not the rule. It is the mismatched premise the councillor treats as if it met the rule."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"**The table matches the harbormaster.** The clerk cites that match as the reason the table is reliable, and cites the table as the reason the harbormaster is reliable.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is an independent calibration of both sources.","It is the conclusion that no further check is needed.","It is a premise in a circle of support.","It is an objection to the harbormaster.","It is a definition of \"tide.\""],"a":2,"e":"The match is offered as support, and the support runs in a circle. It is not an outside calibration. The attractive choice — It is an independent calibration of both sources. — loses because a match looks like a check. The attractive choice calls it independent. The match is one side leaning on the other, not a third instrument."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"The bridge carried ten years of floats. **It will carry this year's float.** The marshal concludes that from the decade alone.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the conclusion.","It is the premise about the past decade.","It is an objection that this float is heavier.","It is a definition of \"harvest.\"","It is evidence that the bridge already failed."],"a":0,"e":"The marshal concludes the bold prediction. The ten years are the support. The attractive choice — It is the premise about the past decade. — loses because the prediction sits next to the decade and is easy to file as more of the record. The decade is the premise. The prediction is the conclusion the marshal draws."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: if a clinic policy's only support is a trial whose volunteers do not match the patients the policy would cover, the policy is not established. The night-clinic policy rests only on a trial of retired volunteers, and it would cover night-shift workers who were not in the trial.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["Every clinic policy is not established.","The night-clinic policy is established.","Retired volunteers cannot give consent.","The night-clinic policy is not established.","Night-shift workers should be excluded from care."],"a":3,"e":"The only support mismatches the patients who would be covered. The principle says that policy is not established. It does not speak about consent or about every policy. The attractive choice — Every clinic policy is not established. — loses because the principle fires on a mismatched trial used as the only support. The attractive choice drops those conditions and condemns every policy. This policy meets the conditions. Others might not."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: if badges are lost for the same reason at two desks staffed in the same way, a clipboard that cut those losses at the first desk is a reason to expect a cut at the second. The craft museum and the science hall lose badges that way, and the clipboard cut the craft museum's losses.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The science hall will lose no badges.","There is a reason to expect the clipboard to cut that kind of loss at the science hall.","The clipboard result is irrelevant to the science hall.","The halls must hire a guard for every desk.","The craft museum should abandon the clipboard."],"a":1,"e":"The desks match in the way the principle requires, and the clipboard cut the loss once. That is a reason to expect a cut, not a guarantee of none. The attractive choice — The science hall will lose no badges. — loses because the principle gives a reason to expect a cut, the same kind of result the craft museum got. Zero losses is a stronger promise than \"a reason to expect a cut.\""},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: a phone ban's success against phone noise is a reason to expect success in a second place only if noise there has the same source. Ward noise is mostly monitors, and phones there are already silent. The ban's only recorded success was against phone noise in a reading room.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The ban will quiet the ward.","The ward's noise is mostly phones.","Reading rooms should be closed.","Monitors are a kind of phone.","The reading-room success is not a reason to expect a quieter ward."],"a":4,"e":"Ward noise is not the phone noise the ban reduced. The principle therefore says the reading-room success is not a reason to expect a quieter ward. The attractive choice — The ban will quiet the ward. — loses because the sources differ, so the principle withholds the expectation. The attractive choice grants the expectation the principle refuses."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: joint occurrence does not, by itself, establish causation. The facts give only that honey sales and the market rose in the same weeks.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The market caused the sales rise.","The market sold no honey.","Those facts do not establish that the market caused the sales rise.","Sales rises are always caused by markets.","The clerk weighed the honey."],"a":2,"e":"Only the joint rise is given, and the principle says that does not establish causation. The attractive choice — The market caused the sales rise. — loses because the principle says this pattern is not enough for causation. The attractive choice is the causation the principle refuses to grant."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The matinee poll is the reviewer's only evidence, and matinee audiences differ from the rest of the house. So the poll does not establish that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["A result about a narrower, differing group does not, by itself, establish the same result about the wider group.","Any majority in any audience establishes the same majority for all audiences.","A play is relaxing if a reviewer says so.","Matinees are the only shows that matter.","Polls are never evidence of anything."],"a":0,"e":"The argument refuses to move from a differing matinee to all theatergoers. That is the narrower-group principle, not a ban on all polls. The attractive choice — Any majority in any audience establishes the same majority for all audiences. — loses because that principle would bless the leap the argument refuses. The argument's direction is that the differing narrower group is not enough. The attractive principle has the same nouns and the opposite rule."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The critic's \"original\" means a new design, and the rule's \"original\" means the first panel. So the critic's word does not show that the rule protects the mural.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["All uses of a word mean the same thing.","Critics decide which murals a town rule protects.","A new design is always the first physical panel.","A conclusion that needs one sense of a word is not established by a premise that uses another sense.","Rules should be ignored when critics disagree."],"a":3,"e":"The argument refuses to let one sense of \"original\" satisfy a rule written in the other sense. That is the principle. The attractive choice — All uses of a word mean the same thing. — loses because if every use meant the same thing, the councillor's inference would go through. The argument's point is that the senses come apart. The attractive principle is the one the argument is rejecting."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"The table and the harbormaster are each cited only for the other. So the table's reliability has not been established.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Agreement between two sources always establishes both, even if each is the only support for the other.","Two claims that rely only on each other do not establish either claim.","Harbormasters are infallible.","Tide tables need no check.","Circular support is the strongest kind of support."],"a":1,"e":"The argument says mutual reliance does not establish reliability. That is the principle, not a claim that agreement of any kind works. The attractive choice — Agreement between two sources always establishes both, even if each is the only support for the other. — loses because the argument treats the closed loop as a failure of proof. The attractive principle treats that same loop as success. The wording is close and the direction is backward."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"The only reason offered is a decade of lighter floats, and this float is heavier. So the decade does not establish that the bridge will carry it.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Past success always establishes the next success.","Heavier floats are always safe.","Parade crowds have been larger in recent years than they were a decade ago.","Ten years is too short to matter, so even a lighter float would prove nothing.","A past success does not establish a future success when the relevant conditions have become harder."],"a":4,"e":"The argument blocks the decade because this float is a harder case. That is a past-success principle with a changed-conditions limit, not a claim that ten years never matter. The attractive choice — Past success always establishes the next success. — loses because the argument refuses the projection because the load got harder. The attractive principle says past success always transfers, which is the rule being denied."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"Umbrella sales in Brine fell during the rainiest month of the year, even though rainier months usually bring higher umbrella sales.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["That month was the rainiest month of the year.","Shops that stayed open stocked more umbrellas that month than in a typical rainy month.","A storm warning kept people home all that month, and the shops that sell umbrellas were among the places they did not visit.","Residents said in a survey that they like walking in heavy rain.","The rain fell mostly at night, when umbrella shops in Brine are closed anyway in every month."],"a":2,"e":"If buyers never reached the shops, sales can fall in a very wet month. The usual rain-and-sales pattern never gets a chance to operate. The attractive choice — That month was the rainiest month of the year. — loses because the rain is one half of the puzzle, restated. A resolution has to let the rain and the low sales sit together. Staying away from the shops does that. Repeating the rainfall does not."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The shop that cut its prices sold fewer units that week than the week before.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The price cut was announced for the following week, and customers waited, so almost no one bought during the announcement week.","Lower prices usually attract more buyers.","The price cut applied to the shop's best-selling items, not only to slow movers.","Competing shops kept their prices unchanged that week.","The shop's advertising that week emphasized the lower prices."],"a":0,"e":"Customers delaying purchases until the cut begins can empty the announcement week even though the coming price is more attractive. The attractive choice — Lower prices usually attract more buyers. — loses because the usual effect of a price cut is the expectation that makes the empty week surprising. Waiting for a cut that has not started explains the empty week. The usual effect does not."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The orchard that installed the recommended netting lost more fruit to birds than in the previous, unnetted year.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Birds eat fruit.","The netting was the brand the extension service recommends for this fruit.","There were fewer birds in the orchard than in the previous year.","A storm tore the netting down in the week the fruit ripened, which was the week the birds fed.","The orchard picked the fruit on the same schedule as in the unnetted year."],"a":3,"e":"If the nets were down during the only week that mattered, installing them earlier does not conflict with a worse loss. The attractive choice — Birds eat fruit. — loses because birds eating fruit is why the netting was supposed to help. It deepens the puzzle. Torn netting during the feeding week explains a worse year despite the installation."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"Students given the longer study guide scored lower on the same quiz than students given the shorter guide.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["One guide had more pages than the other.","The longer guide was handed out ten minutes before the quiz, and the shorter guide had been handed out a week before.","The longer guide covered every topic on the quiz, and the shorter guide skipped two topics.","The two groups had similar scores on a pretest the week before either guide was issued.","Students said they prefer longer guides when they have time to read them."],"a":1,"e":"A week with the short guide can beat ten minutes with the long one. The length stops being an advantage if it arrives too late to be read. The attractive choice — One guide had more pages than the other. — loses because the extra pages are the surprising fact. Timing explains why the longer guide did worse: almost no one could use it. Length alone restates the puzzle."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"After the honor code added more proctors, the school recorded more cheating findings, not fewer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["More proctors were added before the findings rose.","The definition of cheating did not change when the proctors were added.","The number of exams given was the same as in the previous term.","Students were reminded of the honor code more often than before.","The new proctors graded scratch paper the old proctors had ignored, and that paper held cheating the old system missed."],"a":4,"e":"If the new proctors examined paper the old ones skipped, findings can rise because detection rose, not because the code failed. The attractive choice — More proctors were added before the findings rose. — loses because the added proctors are the surprising cause. A new place they looked explains more findings. The staffing increase, restated, does not."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":2,"s":"If a parcel is stored in the river warehouse, it is logged in the blue ledger. Parcel K is not logged in the blue ledger. Therefore, parcel K is not stored in the river warehouse.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["If a painting is in the east gallery, it is framed in oak. This painting is framed in oak. Therefore, it is in the east gallery.","If a painting is in the east gallery, it is framed in oak. This painting is in the east gallery. Therefore, it is framed in oak.","If a painting is in the east gallery, it is framed in oak. This painting is not framed in oak. Therefore, it is not in the east gallery.","Oak frames are heavy. Therefore, gallery paintings are heavy.","This painting is not in the east gallery. Therefore, it is not framed in oak."],"a":2,"e":"Both deny the necessary condition and conclude the sufficient condition is absent. Affirming the oak frame and inferring the gallery is the invalid converse. The attractive choice — If a painting is in the east gallery, it is framed in oak. This painting is framed in oak. Therefore, it is in the east gallery. — loses because the trap uses the same rule and affirms the necessary half, then infers the sufficient half. The stimulus denies the necessary half and infers the denial of the sufficient half, which is what the unframed painting does."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":2,"s":"The shop sold the same number of items, and postcards' share fell. Therefore, fewer postcards were sold.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["The desk received the same number of tickets, and complaints' share fell. Therefore, fewer complaints were received.","Complaints' share fell. Therefore, fewer complaints were received.","Fewer complaints were received. Therefore, complaints' share fell.","The desk received more tickets and complaints' share fell. Therefore, fewer complaints were received.","Postcards are items. Therefore, every item is a postcard."],"a":0,"e":"Both combine an unchanged total with a smaller share and conclude that the number fell. The share alone does not match. The attractive choice — Complaints' share fell. Therefore, fewer complaints were received. — loses because the trap keeps the share drop and drops the fixed total. The stimulus uses both. A share drop alone is the flawed cousin of the valid argument."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":2,"s":"Maintainers match users on the gravel question, and most maintainers want gravel. Therefore, there is reason to think most users want gravel.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["Most people on one crossing want a dawn trip. Therefore, most riders want a dawn trip.","Most riders want a dawn trip. Therefore, the crossing was representative.","Tourists want a dawn trip. Therefore, commuters do not.","The midday crossing matches the ridership in trip purpose, and most people on that crossing want a dawn trip. Therefore, there is reason to think most riders want a dawn trip.","Gravel is cheap. Therefore, users want it."],"a":3,"e":"Both cite a match on the relevant question, a majority in the matched group, and a reason to think the wider group shares that majority. The attractive choice — Most people on one crossing want a dawn trip. Therefore, most riders want a dawn trip. — loses because the trap generalizes without the match. The stimulus generalizes only after stating the match, and so does the crossing choice that includes the match."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"The chef's remark applies no cable standard, and it is the only support offered. Therefore, the remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["A violinist commented on the well. Therefore, the well should be dug deeper.","The violinist's remark applies no well-depth rule, and it is the only support offered for the digging. Therefore, the remark does not establish how deep the well should be dug.","The well manual's author applied the manual. Therefore, the remark is irrelevant to the well.","Chefs bake early. Therefore, cables should be replaced.","Every non-engineer is wrong about wells."],"a":1,"e":"Both say an out-of-field remark is the only support and conclude that the remark does not establish the decision. The attractive choice — A violinist commented on the well. Therefore, the well should be dug deeper. — loses because the trap treats the outsider's remark as establishing the decision. The stimulus says such a remark does not establish it. The well choice that withholds establishment matches. The digging choice does not."},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":3,"s":"If a loaf is on the morning rack, it was baked before dawn. This loaf was baked before dawn. Therefore, it is on the morning rack.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["If a painting is in the east gallery, it is framed in oak. This painting is in the east gallery. Therefore, it is framed in oak.","If a painting is in the east gallery, it is framed in oak. This painting is not framed in oak. Therefore, it is not in the east gallery.","This painting is not in the east gallery. Therefore, it is not framed in oak.","Some oak-framed paintings are not in the east gallery. This painting is oak-framed. Therefore, it is not in the gallery.","If a painting is in the east gallery, it is framed in oak. This painting is framed in oak. Therefore, it is in the east gallery."],"a":4,"e":"Both affirm the necessary condition and infer the sufficient condition. That is the same reversal. The attractive choice — If a painting is in the east gallery, it is framed in oak. This painting is in the east gallery. Therefore, it is framed in oak. — loses because the trap is a valid forward use of the conditional. The stimulus affirms the necessary half and infers the sufficient half. The oak-framed painting that is then placed in the gallery does that. The painting already in the gallery does not."},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":2,"s":"Each map folds into a pocket. Therefore, the packet folds into a pocket.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["Each pane is light enough to lift, and the stack is a single pane. Therefore, the stack is light enough to lift.","The stack is light enough to lift. Therefore, each pane is.","Each pane is light enough to lift. Therefore, the stack of panes is light enough to lift.","The packet is a map. Therefore, the map is a packet.","No one reported a heavy map. Therefore, no map is heavy."],"a":2,"e":"Both give the whole a feature shown only for the parts, with no further premise. One pane would cure the flaw, so it is not the parallel. The attractive choice — Each pane is light enough to lift, and the stack is a single pane. Therefore, the stack is light enough to lift. — loses because the trap adds the single-pane premise that makes the inference safe. The stimulus and the stack-of-panes choice lack that premise and still conclude about the whole."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"Most passengers on one midday crossing want a dawn trip. The scheduler concludes that most of the ferry's passengers want one.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether that midday crossing matches the ridership in trip purpose and how often they ride","Whether a few passengers on the crossing said no","Whether the ferry is painted white","Whether dawn comes before noon","Whether the scheduler has a desk"],"a":0,"e":"The argument stands or falls on whether the crossing represents the ridership. Paint and a few dissenters do not decide that. The attractive choice — Whether a few passengers on the crossing said no — loses because a few no votes are already compatible with \"most said yes,\" so either answer leaves the generalization as it stands. A mismatch with the ridership would sink it, and a match would support it."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"No log mentions a jam. The director concludes that the motor has not jammed.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether a jammed motor is expensive to replace","Whether the motor is louder than the fan","Whether visitors ask about the dome","Whether any jam would have been entered in a log that is complete for this period","Whether the director drinks coffee"],"a":3,"e":"Silence supports \"no jam\" only if a jam would have been logged. Cost does not answer that question. The attractive choice — Whether a jammed motor is expensive to replace — loses because expense can be high or low without making the silence more or less informative. A log that would have caught every jam would support the director, and a log that would not would empty the argument."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":3,"s":"The critic called the design \"original,\" and the rule protects an \"original\" panel. The councillor concludes that the mural is protected.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the heritage rule is more than a century old","Whether the critic's sense of \"original\" is the rule's sense","Whether the critic publishes on Fridays","Whether murals can be large","Whether the councillor likes the color of the paint"],"a":1,"e":"The argument is an equivocation unless the senses match. Age, publication day, and taste do not test the match. The attractive choice — Whether the heritage rule is more than a century old — loses because the rule's age can vary without aligning or separating the two senses. Matching senses would support the councillor, and different senses would sink the councillor."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":3,"s":"A pastry chef says the cables should be replaced. The board concludes that they should be replaced.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the chef has won cooking awards","Whether the bridge is downtown","Whether the cables are steel","Whether the board meets on Tuesdays","Whether the chef is applying a relevant cable standard or speaking outside that field"],"a":4,"e":"The citation is only as good as its relevance. Awards do not test relevance. The standard the chef did or did not apply does. The attractive choice — Whether the chef has won cooking awards — loses because awards can pile up without making the remark evidence, and their absence would not by itself answer a remark that did apply a cable standard. A relevant standard would help the board, and a purely culinary basis would not."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":2,"s":"Ade: The market caused the honey-sales rise, since the two happened in the same weeks. Bo: The two happened in the same weeks, but the clover bloom, not the market, caused the rise.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["honey sales and the market rose in the same weeks","honey was offered for sale","the market caused the honey-sales rise","there was a weekend market","Ade and Bo are talking about weeks rather than years"],"a":2,"e":"Ade credits the market. Bo credits the bloom. The shared weeks are not in dispute. The attractive choice — honey sales and the market rose in the same weeks — loses because both accept the overlap. Bo uses it and then names a different cause. The dispute is the cause, not the calendar."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":2,"s":"Greta: The garage holds fewer bikes, because bikes fell from 40 percent of vehicles to 25 percent. Hiro: The share fell, but the garage now holds twice the vehicles, so the share drop does not show there are fewer bikes.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the drop in share shows that the garage holds fewer bikes","bikes' share of the vehicles fell","the garage holds vehicles","40 percent is greater than 25 percent","Greta and Hiro mentioned bikes"],"a":0,"e":"Greta says the share drop shows fewer bikes. Hiro says it does not. The share itself is not the issue. The attractive choice — bikes' share of the vehicles fell — loses because both accept the share drop. Hiro adds the doubled total and denies that the drop shows a smaller number. The dispute is what the drop shows, not whether it happened."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":2,"s":"Priya: This year's heavier float will be carried, because the bridge carried every lighter float for a decade. Quinn: The decade covered only lighter floats, so it does not show the bridge will carry this heavier one.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["this year's float is heavier than the earlier floats","the bridge carried lighter floats for a decade","floats can cross bridges","the decade of lighter floats shows that the bridge will carry this heavier float","Priya and Quinn mentioned a decade"],"a":3,"e":"Priya says the decade shows success with this float. Quinn says it does not. The weight comparison is shared. The attractive choice — this year's float is heavier than the earlier floats — loses because both treat this float as heavier. The attractive choice is that agreed comparison. They disagree on whether the decade shows the bridge will carry it."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Vela: The lunchtime walks caused the fatigue drop at the offices that added them. Wes: Those offices also cut workloads in half, and the other offices did not, so the walks are not shown to be the cause.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the walk offices later had fewer fatigue complaints","the walks are shown to be the cause of the fatigue drop","there were offices that did not add walks","fatigue can drop","Vela and Wes discussed offices"],"a":1,"e":"Vela says the walks are the cause. Wes says that has not been shown, because of the workload cut. The later complaints are not the point of conflict. The attractive choice — the walk offices later had fewer fatigue complaints — loses because Wes does not deny the later gap. Wes denies that the walks are shown to be its cause. The gap can be shared. The causal claim cannot."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Post hoc","d":3,"s":"The gallery hung a new curtain on Friday, and ticket sales fell on Saturday. No other change in price, weather, or programming that weekend is described, and the manager has not compared other weekends.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The curtain must have caused the Saturday drop.","The curtain had no effect on sales.","Ticket sales fall only after curtains are changed.","The gallery should take the curtain down.","The curtain is a candidate explanation for the Saturday drop, not a cause the facts have isolated."],"a":4,"e":"The order is real and unexplained by any described rival, so the curtain is a candidate. The same gap keeps it from being a must-have cause, a proven non-cause, or a reason for a business order. The attractive choice — The curtain must have caused the Saturday drop. — loses because Friday-then-Saturday makes the curtain a candidate. \"Must\" treats the order as isolation. With no comparison and no claim that nothing else changed in the town, the facts do not isolate the curtain."}
]);
